timos \"difíciles\" puntúan 0,711 y 0,768: por *encima* de la mejor evasión (0,701). Las clases se solapan; el \"+0,030\" existe solo tras apartar 2 puntos a un cajón de \"FP declarados\" por decisión del propio autor del umbral. La justificación (\"la causa no está en el texto\") no está testeada. | La solapación es aritmética; la legitimidad de la exclusión, sin verificar. | Test ciego: los 2 difíciles + juicios + cierres mezclados, entregados a anotadores independientes con solo el texto. Si separan mejor que el azar, la señal SÍ está en el texto y falla el cerco; si no, la exclusión se sostiene. Recomputar el margen con los difíciles dentro: es negativo. |\n| 3 | **n=5 no acota la cola legítima.** El 0,671 es el máximo de 5 cierres ordinarios: estima un cuantil ~p83, no un p99. Y los 2 difíciles ya demuestran que texto legítimo puede vivir en 0,711–0,768. La frontera legítima no está estimada; está ilustrada. | Casi seguro, estadísticamente. | Máximo acumulado por lotes de ~50 cierres reales hasta estancarse (≥3 lotes sin récord); histograma de producción en 0,60–0,72 para el p99 real. |\n| 4 | **Precisión de teatro.** El punto medio de dos extremos (n=5, n=9, un solo atacante) tiene un ruido de jackknife del orden de ±0,015. La incertidumbre del procedimiento es **mayor que la diferencia que se debate** (0,011). Escribir \"0,686\" implica una precisión de ±0,001 que nada respalda. | Computable con los datos ya recogidos. | Jackknife y bootstrap del punto medio sobre el banco; reportar intervalo. Si el intervalo al 95% es más ancho que 0,011, la elección es ruido con nombre. |\n| 5 | **El adversario es adaptativo; 0,701 no es un suelo.** Es el mejor intento de *un* atacante sin conocer el umbral y sin iterar. Desplegado, el sistema responde un bit por consulta y el atacante converge. Además, si el embedding es a nivel de documento, el relleno ERP/QA/KPI es dilución: la similitud de una absolución con 90% de relleno no está medida. | Desconocido: no existe ni una medición. | Curva de dilución (sentencia + 0/25/50/75/90% de relleno, graficar similitud). Red team adaptativo con acceso de consulta y umbral congelado; contar iteraciones hasta bajar de 0,686. Si la dilución llega a 0,5, el debate 0,686-vs-0,697 es muebles de cubierta. |\n| 6 | **Punto medio = estética, no punto de operación.** El cerco solo escala: FN catastrófico, FP = una llamada. Bajo esa asimetría, el umbral óptimo viene del coste del FN, del coste del FP y de las tasas base; ninguna de las tres cosas fue estimada. El punto medio asume colas simétricas; la adversaria es adaptativa y la legítima estacionaria. | La asimetría está en la especificación; los costes, no medidos. | Volumen de producción en 0,65–0,70 (cuántas llamadas/semana añade 0,686 frente a 0,697 o 0,66); coste explícito de un FN; derivar el umbral de la función de pérdida y ver dónde cae — quizá ni cerca de 0,686. |\n| 7 | **El segundo cerco no ha hecho nada y su independencia no está probada.** Cero discrepancias en 22 casos = B no cambió un solo resultado del banco. En OR solo aporta si atrapa lo que A deja pasar, y ambos modelos son de la misma familia/proveedor: los ataques que evaden A probablemente transfieren a B (fallo de modo común). | La falta de efecto es dato; la correlación, no medida. | Ataques de transferencia: evasiones optimizadas contra A con oráculo de A, puntuadas con B. Correlación A–B sobre corpus grande. Si transfieren, B es decorativo y discutir su umbral es discutir la pintura de un poste que no sostiene nada. |\n| 8 | **Calibración monolingüe en un modelo multilingüe.** El banco es español; un embedding multilingüe tiene offsets por idioma. Si la producción firma en más de un idioma, 0,686 puede no transferir. | Desconocido. | Replicar el banco por cada idioma real de producción. Si el máximo legítimo se mueve ±0,03 por idioma, un número único está mal especificado y la decisión ni siquiera está bien planteada. |\n| 9 | **\"0 molestias\" es una definición, no una medición.** El resultado honesto del banco es 2/7 cierres legítimos escalados (29%). \"0 molestias\" vale solo para el subconjunto definido para excluir las molestias. Y el FN en producción es invisible (no dispara alerta): \"9/9\" no dice nada de la tasa de caza una vez desplegado. | La tasa del banco es aritmética; la de producción, desconocida. | Piloto: tasa semanal de escaladas reales + auditoría de calidad de las revisiones humanas (¿rechazan algo alguna vez o solo sellan?). Juicios sintéticos inyectados en vivo para medir la tasa de caza real. |\n\n## Razonamiento\n\n**El núcleo: la validación tiene resolución cero exactamente en la magnitud que se decide.** El intervalo (0,671; 0,701] produce, para *cualquier* umbral dentro de él, el mismo resultado publicado. La decisión entre 0,686 y 0,697 es una diferencia de 0,011 dentro de una meseta empírica de 0,030. Es medir con una regla graduada en centímetros para fijar una posición dentro de tres milímetros. El \"9/9\" no confirma 0,686: confirma que el banco es *compatible* con 0,686, como lo es con media docena de valores igual de defendibles. Circularidad sería lo de menos; aquí directamente no hay señal.\n\n**El margen está manufacturado.** El \"+0,030\" solo existe después de sacar del cómputo dos textos legítimos que puntúan *por encima* de la mejor evasión. Sin ese cajón, el margen es negativo y la pregunta correcta no es \"¿dónde está el punto medio?\" sino \"¿cuántas molestias por semana se aceptan?\". La decisión responde la pregunta geométrica porque la respuesta a la operativa exigiría medir la producción, y no se midió.\n\n**Dirección defendible, magnitud arbitraria.** Bajar el umbral en vez de subirlo era razonable en dirección: FN catastrófico, FP barato, y 0,697 deja solo 0,004 de aire bajo la evasión más floja. Pero la *magnitud* —exactamente la mitad del margen observado— no viene de ningún coste sino de la bisagra. El punto medio es neutro solo si las dos colas futuras son simétricas. No lo son: la adversaria crece con el esfuerzo del atacante (y 0,701 es el mejor intento de una sola persona sin iterar ni conocer el objetivo), la legítima es estacionaria. Con colas asimétricas, el punto medio no es neutral: es generoso con el atacante. El punto de parada defendible es el presupuesto de molestias humanas —no medido—, no la mediatriz de dos extremos de muestra mínima.\n\n**¿Habría sido mejor 0,697?** Indecidible con estos datos, y esa es la acusación. La banda decisiva [0,686; 0,697) tiene cero muestras; ambas cifras caen dentro de la meseta. Cuando la decisión dice \"un umbral que solo separa los casos fáciles no separa nada\", describe esa banda vacía como si estuviera poblada de evasiones. Podría estar poblada de cierres legítimos del tipo \"se libera el recurso bloqueado\" (que ya está a 0,015 del umbral, no a años luz). Afirmar la composición de una banda sin muestras no es un argumento: es un prior disfrazado de resultado. Ni 0,686 ni 0,697 tienen hoy un solo dato de su lado.\n\n**Sobre las cero discrepancias:** confirman exactamente que B no ha aportado nada medible, y no prueban que sea seguro. En OR, el segundo cerco vale solo donde falla el primero, y dos embeddings de la misma familia tienen modos de fallo correlacionados. El tercer problema existe: la defensa común de ambos cercos contra un adversario adaptativo es una única hipótesis sin test (ataque 7).\n\n**Qué me haría cambiar el veredicto:** (a) composición de clase de la banda 0,680–0,700, muestreada con evasiones dirigidas y cierres reales; (b) máximo legítimo estabilizado por lotes; (c) red team adaptativo con acceso de consulta y umbral congelado; (d) ataques de transferencia A→B. Sin (a) y (c), 0,686 no es una decisión validada: es una apuesta presentada como resultado, tomada con la regla equivocada sobre un margen que solo existe por exclusión manual.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"Let me think carefully about this task. The user wants me to attack a specific decision: choosing threshold 0.686 for model B instead of 0.697, based on a bank of 22 cases where the margin between the hardest-to-catch evasion (0.701) and the most-similar legitimate closure (0.671) is only 0.030.\n\nThe output contract is strict:\n- First line: verdict SOSTENIBLE / NO SOSTENIBLE (about the decision I'm attacking — i.e., whether the decision to use 0.686 survives my attacks)\n- Then a numbered table of attacks with columns \"¿es cierto?\" (is it true?) and \"qué habría que medir para saberlo\" (what would need to be measured to know)\n- Reasoning after, if I want, max 2000 words total.\n- I'm a mechanism dissenter: my only job is to try to knock down this ONE decision. No praise, no context summary, no cosmetic improvements.\n\nLet me think about the substance.\n\nThe decision: threshold for model B = 0.686, midpoint of the \"complete bank\" margin (0.671 to 0.701), rather than 0.697 (midpoint of the narrow bank).\n\nAttack angles:\n\n**Attack 1: The 0.030 margin is not a separation, it's a coin flip zone.**\n\nThe gap between the highest legitimate ordinary closure (0.671) and the lowest evasion (0.701) is 0.030 in cosine similarity. Key question: what's the test-retest / stability of these embeddings? Embedding models produce deterministic outputs for identical text, so the numbers themselves are stable. But the question is generalization: with 5 ordinary legitimate closures and 9 adversarial texts, we have essentially 5 samples of the \"legitimate high\" distribution and 9 of the \"adversarial low\" distribution. The extreme values (max of 5, min of 9) are order statistics with enormous variance. The true 99th percentile of legitimate closures could easily be above 0.686; the true 1st percentile of adversarial attacks could be below 0.686.\n\nActually, more subtle: the attacker wrote 9 texts with the explicit brief to evade. But that attacker is one attacker, one imagination, one session. Adversarial coverage is inherently unbounded — the 0.701 is the best of ONE attacker's 9 attempts. The claim \"9/9 evasions cazadas\" only covers evasions of the type this attacker generated. A different attacker (or the same one with the threshold now deployed, i.e., adaptive adversary who can query the system) can iterate.\n\nThis is the fundamental problem: threshold set on a fixed bank vs. adaptive adversary. Once 0.686 is deployed, the attacker's optimization target is known (or discoverable through probing). The margin of 0.030 gives the adversary a very narrow but real target: get a judgment-like text below 0.686. The defender's samples don't bound this.\n\nWhat measurement would confirm/refute: hold-out testing with fresh attackers (multiple independent attackers, multiple rounds, ideally with the threshold hidden or with oracle feedback to simulate adaptive attack), measure the distribution of evasion scores; measure the distribution of legitimate closure scores from production logs (hundreds/thousands), get the real tail (p99, p99.9) of legitimate similarity, not the max of 5.\n\n**Attack 2: Circularity — threshold selected and validated on the same 22 points.**\n\nThe threshold was chosen AS the midpoint of the observed margin on the complete bank. Then the \"result\" 9/9 evasions and 0 annoyances is reported on the same bank. Of course it passes: the threshold was constructed to make exactly those points fall on the right sides. The midpoint construction guarantees, by construction, that (max legitimate + min evasion)/2 separates them, provided the margin is positive. So \"9/9, 0 molestias\" is not an out-of-sample measurement at all; it's a tautology of the selection procedure. The only quasi-information is the 2 declared false positives, which were known pre-hoc.\n\nEven the \"margin +0.030\" is an in-sample statistic. With n=5 and n=9, the in-sample margin is optimistically biased: selection of extremes.\n\nWhat measurement: k-fold / leave-one-out cross-validation on the bank, but more honestly, a fresh bank written by people other than the system author, with the threshold frozen before testing. Report confusion matrix out-of-sample. Also report the stability of the threshold under bootstrap resampling of the bank: if you resample the 22 cases with replacement and recompute the midpoint, what's the distribution of the chosen threshold? If the 95% interval spans, say, 0.66–0.71, then the third decimal of 0.686 is theater.\n\nBootstrap is a great concrete measurement here. With max of 5 legitimate points and min of 9 adversarial points, jackknife: remove the 0.671 text — the midpoint jumps to... if the next-highest legitimate is, say, 0.64, midpoint becomes (0.64+0.701)/2 = 0.67ish. Remove the 0.701 evasion — midpoint rises. The threshold's sensitivity to single cases is the real fragility indicator.\n\n**Attack 3: The 0.015 razor.**\n\nThe closest legitimate text is at 0.671 (0.015 below threshold); the weakest evasion at 0.701 (0.015 above). Both within measurement noise of... well, embeddings are deterministic, but the SPACE of future texts is not. The 0.671 text (\"Se libera el recurso bloqueado por el proceso anterior y la cola vuelve a drenar con normalidad, según el panel adjunto\") is a paraphrase family — legitimate ops language about releasing/blocked/normal that lives right at the frontier. Any legitimate closure that mentions \"liberation,\" \"freed,\" \"cleared,\" \"resolved without penalty\" will land in this band. Similarly, an evasion needs only to shave 0.015 more.\n\nKey point about the direction of asymmetry: the fence only escalates to human; it never authorizes. So a false negative (missed judgment → machine signs a judgment document) is the catastrophic error; a false positive (human called unnecessarily) is the cost. The decision lowered the threshold from 0.697 to 0.686 — moving it 0.011 down toward the legitimate tail — precisely to catch evasions. That's the right direction for safety given the asymmetry (lower threshold = more human escalations = safer). Wait — actually, this is the one thing that DEFENDS the decision. Lower threshold = more conservative = more human review. Since the fence can only escalate, false positives cost human time, false negatives cost the whole premise of the system.\n\nHmm, but I'm supposed to attack. Can I attack the direction? The stated rationale \"un umbral que solo separa los casos fáciles no separa nada\" — the argument for 0.686 over 0.697 is that 0.697 would have missed the 0.701 evasion? Wait no: 0.697 < 0.701, so threshold 0.697 would still catch the 0.701 evasion. Let me check: threshold is \"if similarity ≥ threshold, require human.\" Evasion at 0.701 with threshold 0.697: 0.701 > 0.697, so it WOULD be caught. So why prefer 0.686?\n\nThe rationale: \"un umbral que solo separa los casos fáciles no separa nada\" — 0.697 is the midpoint of the NARROW bank (0.616 max non-judgment, 0.778 min judgment). But the complete bank shows an evasion at 0.701, only 0.004 above 0.697. So with 0.697, the margin to the weakest evasion is 0.004 — essentially zero. And to the legitimate 0.671, the margin is 0.026. So 0.686 balances: 0.015 to each side. Midpoint of the true observed extremes. That's the argument: 0.697 sits 0.004 from the observed adversarial floor, so any new evasion that's slightly weaker passes under it, while there's 0.026 of wasted margin on the legitimate side.\n\nBut the counter-attack: the midpoint is only optimal if you believe the future distributions are symmetric around the observed extremes — i.e., that the next evasion is as likely to be 0.016 weaker as the next legitimate is 0.016 stronger. That's an unstated and almost certainly false assumption:\n- The adversarial side is ADAPTIVE and unbounded: an attacker optimizing against a known model can, with enough iterations, drive similarity arbitrarily low (there's literature on adversarial examples against embeddings/semantics; paraphrase attacks, translation round-trips, adding irrelevant technical filler). The adversarial distribution's tail extends far below 0.701 with nonzero probability that grows with attacker effort.\n- The legitimate side is roughly stationary: ops language doesn't adversarially optimize toward higher similarity. Its tail is what it is.\n\nGiven asymmetric tail behavior, the optimal threshold is NOT the midpoint; it should be pushed DOWN toward the legitimate tail — as low as tolerable operational cost allows. Wait, that argues for even lower than 0.686! Interesting. If false positives are cheap (human gets called) and false negatives are catastrophic, and the adversary can push below any fixed threshold given effort, then... the whole threshold approach degrades: the real defense is that the adversary's constraint is that the document must still MEAN a judgment to its readers (the signature must be legally effective). The attacker can't push semantic similarity arbitrarily low while keeping the text a valid judgment to the human counterpart. Hmm, but actually they can somewhat: euphemism. \"El expediente queda en paz\" etc. Actually the evasion texts already use ERP/QA/KPI filler — that's dilution.\n\nWait, but there's a subtle point: the system's escape is that the fence can only escalate. So the question \"is 0.686 better than 0.697?\" — the answer from safety asymmetry: lower is safer. So attacking the DIRECTION of the choice (down vs up) is weak; attacking the PRECISION and the PROCEDURE is strong.\n\nActually, can I attack the decision more fundamentally? The decision is \"0.686 because midpoint of complete bank, not 0.697\". Attack lines:\n\n(a) The midpoint heuristic is unexamined: midpoint assumes equal and symmetric generalization tails. No evidence. With an adaptive adversary, the adversarial tail is heavier. If you believe adversarial tails are heavier, you'd go LOWER than 0.686 — but then you eat more false positives on legitimate ops text near 0.671... but false positives only cost a human call. Actually the decision could be attacked from the \"you didn't go low enough / you stopped at an aesthetic midpoint rather than an argued operating point\" angle: the threshold should be chosen by a cost model (cost of missed judgment vs cost of human call), not by geometric symmetry. The midpoint is chosen because it's pretty, not because anyone measured the ratio of costs.\n\n(b) The 22-case bank: author-written narrow bank (12, only 6 reach signature — so the \"no-judgment\" samples in the narrow bank are... wait, the narrow bank has judgments min 0.778 max 0.916 and non-judgments min 0.502 max 0.616; the 6 that reach signature are presumably the judgment ones? Hmm, \"solo 6 llegan hasta la firma\" — of 12 cases, 6 reach signature). Then complete bank adds 9 attacker texts + 5 ordinary legit + 2 difficult legit = 22 total? 12 + 9 + 5 + 2 = 28, but they say 22 cases with zero discrepancies. Hmm: \"CERO discrepancias entre los dos cercos en los 22 casos\". Maybe the 22 = 12 narrow + 9 evasions... no wait. Let me not over-litigate arithmetic; maybe some overlap. Actually: narrow 12 (of which 6 reach signature), plus 9 + 5 + 2 = 16, total 28. The \"22\" might be a different subset. This arithmetic inconsistency is itself an attack point — the reported N doesn't reconcile, which matters when your entire margin is 0.030 and your sample is single digits. Hmm, but maybe the 22 = 6 + 9 + 5 + 2 = 22! Yes: the 6 signature-reaching cases + 9 evasions + 5 ordinary + 2 difficult = 22. That reconciles. So the complete-bank \"margin\" computation used... the narrow bank's judgment min 0.778? But the complete bank says the lowest-similarity judgment is the evasion at 0.701. And the highest ordinary legit is 0.671. OK.\n\nSo the legitimate-side sample for the frontier is: 5 ordinary closures + maybe the 6 narrow cases (which are low similarity, 0.502–0.616 for non-judgments... wait the narrow non-judgments go up to 0.616, below 0.671). So the legitimate distribution near the frontier is represented by exactly FIVE ordinary closures. The p95 of legitimate similarity is estimated from n=5. That's the statistical heart: your 0.015 margin below threshold is 1/3 of the observed sample's inter-extreme range... The extreme of 5 samples estimates a quantile around 5/6 ≈ 83rd percentile, not a tail bound. The real p99 of legitimate closures could be 0.69, 0.70, we have no idea.\n\n(c) The two \"difficult on purpose\" cases at 0.768 and 0.711 counted as declared false positives. 0.711 is ABOVE the weakest evasion (0.701)! So a legitimate difficult closure scores higher than the best adversarial evasion. This destroys the notion of a margin: the classes overlap once you admit the difficult legitimates. The decision handles this by declaring them (\"the distinguishing cause isn't in the text\"). Fine — but then the reported \"margin +0.030\" is a margin computed AFTER excluding inconvenient points from the classes. The margin is conditional on a classification decision made by the same author who chose the threshold. If you include the 0.711 difficult legitimate in the \"legitimate\" class, the margin is NEGATIVE (0.711 > 0.701): there is no separating threshold, period. The system's answer is \"those cases are semantically indistinguishable from judgments; the distinguishing information isn't in the text; we accept 2 FPs.\" OK, but then: (i) the choice of which points to exclude determines the margin, and the exclusion was made by the system's author — same circularity; (ii) if legitimate difficult closures routinely land at 0.711–0.768, the production false-positive rate is not \"0 molestias\" but something driven by the base rate of such phrasings, unknown from n=2. And more importantly: if a legitimate text can reach 0.768, then an attacker has 0.768 as an EXISTENCE PROOF that a legitimate-looking, machine-signable text can score 0.768 — wait no, the direction: high score → human required. The attacker wants LOW score. The difficult legitimates show high scores are attainable by legit text; that doesn't help