eement is fine in OR configuration (any flag escalates). The real question for a fence is FALSE NEGATIVES of both simultaneously (common-cause failure). Two embeddings models from... gemini-embedding-001 (Google) and text-multilingual-embedding-002 (also Google? \"text-multilingual-embedding-002\" is Vertex AI's multilingual embedding). Both from the same vendor, possibly similar training data/architecture family → correlated failures. Common-mode failure: the disjunction of two correlated detectors ≈ one detector. The measurement: adversarial texts optimized against model A only, then test whether model B catches them (transfer attack). If attacks transfer (they usually do within-family), the second fence is decorative. This is a key \"third problem\" the prompt hints at (\"¿hay un tercer problema?\").\n\n(e) Threshold scales: \"its cosine scale is different so reusing 0.70 would be inventing a number\" — fine, but then computing a midpoint of a 0.030 margin and reporting to three decimals (0.686) implies precision of ±0.001 that nothing in the procedure supports. Sensitivity analysis: how do the two candidate thresholds (0.686 vs 0.697) actually differ in outcomes? On this bank: both catch all 9 evasions (weakest 0.701 > 0.697). Both leave the 5 ordinary alone (highest 0.671 < 0.686 < 0.697). Both trigger the 2 difficult FPs. So ON THIS BANK THE DECISION IS A NO-OP: 0.686 and 0.697 produce IDENTICAL outcomes on all 22 cases! The only difference is hypothetical future cases falling between 0.686 and 0.697 — a 0.011 band with ZERO observed samples. The decision commits to covering an unobserved band based on... the claim that future evasions might land in [0.686, 0.697). But equally, future legitimate closures might land there too, and 0.686 flags them. Given no data in the band, the choice between 0.686 and 0.697 is a prior-driven bet, not an empirical result. The stated rationale (\"separa algo\") is unfalsifiable on current data. THE KEY MEASUREMENT: produce cases specifically in the 0.686–0.697 band (both classes) and see which class populates it. If it's populated by legitimate texts, 0.686 was wrong; if by evasions, right. This is a sharp attack: the decision is about a region with zero data points, and the bank cannot arbitrate it.\n\nWait, let me double check: does 0.697 catch the 0.701 evasion? 0.701 ≥ 0.697 → yes, caught, by 0.004. And the difficult legitimates at 0.711 and 0.768 trigger under both. The ordinary legit at 0.671 is below both. So indeed identical outcomes on all 22. The decision's actual effect is entirely deferred to unseen data. Excellent attack — it reframes: \"the measured result 9/9, 0 molestias is IDENTICAL under the rejected alternative, so the measured result provides zero evidence for choosing 0.686 over 0.697.\" The only differential evidence is the argument that the 0.004 margin at 0.697 is too thin — which is an argument about the future, i.e., about the adversarial tail. And if you believe adversarial tails are heavy, symmetry says push lower still — why stop at the midpoint? The midpoint is defended by symmetry, but the adversary is not symmetric with legitimate text. Under cost asymmetry (FN catastrophic, FP = a phone call), the rational move is: set threshold as low as the human-review budget tolerates. Nobody measured the FP cost or budget. The choice of midpoint is geometric aesthetics, not risk analysis.\n\nHmm, but careful: lowering the threshold indefinitely eventually flags ordinary ops text (0.671, then 0.64...). The budget constraint is real. But it's UNMEASURED. So the attack: \"0.686 was chosen by a symmetry heuristic; the two quantities that actually determine the optimum (cost of a missed judgment, tolerable rate of human escalations on legitimate volume) were never estimated; therefore the operating point is not derived from the system's loss function.\" Measurement: estimate production base rates — what fraction of daily documents are near-frontier legit (0.65–0.69)? If 5%