was not tested — it's asserted. Measurement: blind test — give the 2 difficult texts (plus judgments and ordinary) to independent annotators with only the text; if annotators can't separate them better than chance, the \"cause isn't in the text\" claim holds; if they can, the text DOES contain the signal and the fence (not the ontology) is what's failing.\n\nThat's a nice one.\n\n(k) Also the \"6 of 12 reach signature\" — half the narrow bank never exercises the fence's decision path. Selection of what's in the bank is again author-driven.\n\n(l) Question 4 in the prompt: \"cero discrepancias = safe confirmation or proof of no added value?\" I covered: on this bank B is redundant; the disjunction only helps if A and B fail independently; both are Google-family embeddings, plausibly trained on similar corpora → correlated; and the OR only adds value for cases where B catches what A misses — zero observed. So B's marginal benefit is unmeasured, and its COST is measured: B at 0.686 adds false positives... wait does it? In OR, B triggering adds escalation. On this bank, zero discrepancies means B never triggered differently... hmm, \"zero discrepancies between the two fences\" — meaning on all 22, both fences gave the same verdict? Then B changed nothing at all on the bank: no added catches, no added FPs. So the entire second-fence project has, to date, changed zero outcomes on zero cases, and the threshold debate 0.686 vs 0.697 also changed zero outcomes on those cases. The decision under attack is thus empirically unconstrained by all evidence gathered. The evidence cannot refute ANY threshold in (0.671, 0.701) — wait, actually any threshold in (0.671, 0.701] would give 9/9 and 0 annoyances... and also any threshold in (0.616, 0.671] would too (narrow non-judgments max 0.616)... So the observation \"9/9, 0 molestias\" is consistent with any threshold in (0.616, 0.701] roughly. The evidence is compatible with a whole interval of thresholds; the choice within that interval is pure prior. THE core circularity result: the validation cannot distinguish 0.62 from 0.686 from 0.70. It only rules out >0.701 (misses evasion) and ≤0.616 (misses... wait no). Hmm: threshold below 0.616 → ordinary non-judgments from narrow bank trigger FP. So valid interval for \"9/9 + 0 molestias on ordinary\" is (0.671... let me redo: to have 0 annoyances on the 5 ordinary, need threshold > 0.671. To catch all 9 evasions, need threshold ≤ 0.701. So (0.671, 0.701]. Any of those ~0.030-wide intervals of thresholds produces the identical \"result\". The specific pick of 0.686 within it is determined by the midpoint rule, and the debate 0.686 vs 0.697 is INSIDE this empirically indistinguishable interval. So: the measurement performed has zero power to arbitrate the decision. That's the cleanest possible statement of attack 2. The validation is not circular in the sense of overfitting the number — it's that the validation has NO RESOLUTION at the scale of the decision. Like measuring with a ruler marked in centimeters to choose a position within 3 mm.\n\nGood. Now assemble attacks (aim for maybe 7–9 attacks, numbered, each with \"¿es cierto?\" and \"qué medir\"):\n\nA1. Margen 0.030 = extremes of n=5 and n=9 → order statistics, no tail bound; true overlap already demonstrated by the 2 difficult legitimates at 0.711/0.768 sitting ABOVE the best evasion. The \"margin\" exists only after author-declared exclusions. ¿Es cierto? Likely — the exclusion was asserted, not tested. Medir: blind annotation test on the 2 difficult texts + matched judgments; bootstrap/jackknife of the margin; production histogram of legit similarities to estimate real p99.\n\nA2. Circular / zero-resolution validation: any threshold in (0.671, 0.701] yields the identical 9/9 + 0 molestias; therefore the measured \"result\" contains no information about 0.686 vs 0.697 vs 0.65 vs 0.70; both candidate thresholds produce identical outcomes on all 22 cases; the decision operates entirely on unseen data. Medir: enumerate outcomes across the threshold sweep on the bank (show flat region); collect/author cases inside the (0.686, 0.697) band and inside (0.671, 0.701) generally to see which class populates it.\n\nA3. Adaptive adversary + dilution: 0.701 is one attacker's best without knowledge of the threshold or model; deployed system is probeable (oracle: does it demand human or not → 1 bit per query); attacker iterates until below threshold; dilution with filler scales with attacker-controlled length. If doc-level embedding, dilution can go far below 0.686. Medir: chunking/dilution curve (judgment sentence + X% filler, plot score); adaptive red-team with query access and frozen threshold; measure how many iterations to drop below 0.686.\n\nA4. Midpoint heuristic ≠ risk-based operating point: fence can only escalate; FN catastrophic, FP cheap(er). Midpoint assumes symmetric tails (future evasion as likely at 0.685 as future legit at 0.687). Adversarial tail is heavier than legit tail → if anything, push lower; but how much lower depends on FP budget, which was never estimated. Decision is geometric aesthetics, not loss-function optimization. ¿Es cierto? The asymmetry is in the spec (fence only escalates); the budget is unmeasured. Medir: production volume of near-frontier legit docs (how many human calls does 0.686 vs 0.697 vs 0.66 add per week?); cost/consequence estimate of a machine-signed judgment; then choose threshold from loss, and see where it lands.\n\nA5. Second fence redundancy + common-mode: zero discrepancies in 22 cases means B changed no outcome on the bank; OR-configuration adds value only under independent failures; both models are Google-family, plausibly correlated; attacks on A likely transfer to B. ¿Cierto? Unknown — untested. Medir: transfer attacks — optimize evasions against model A (with oracle access to A alone), then score under B; also compute score correlation between A and B across a large corpus; if r is very high, B is decorative and its threshold debate is moot.\n\nA6. Single-attacker, author-authored bank: 9 evasions from one mind, narrow bank from the author's mind, 2 difficult legitimates imagined by the author. Coverage is a function of two imaginations. ¿Cierto? Structurally evident. Medir: N independent red-teamers (≥3–5), different native languages/registers; longitudinal production sampling of real documents; recompute the extreme values and the margin each round — does 0.701 survive as the floor?\n\nA7. Language/regional invariance: multilingual embedding, Spanish-only calibration; per-language offsets can shift both class distributions differently, so 0.686 may not transfer. ¿Cierto? Unknown. Medir: replicate bank per production language; test whether one threshold holds or per-language quantile calibration is needed.\n\nA8. The declared-FP handling hides the real operating cost: 2/7 legit closures (2 difficult of 7 total legit in complete bank? — 5 ordinary + 2 difficult = 7 legit) are FPs = 28.6% FP rate on legit closures in the bank; production base rate unknown; risk of alert fatigue → human rubber-stamp → fence nominal. ¿Cierto? The 2/7 is arithmetic from the bank; production rate unknown. Medir: pilot in production, measure weekly escalation rate, and quality of human decisions over time (audit sample of escalations — do humans ever \"reject\" i.e., find judgments? if humans never reject anything, the human is decorative and the FN protection is illusory).\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtle thing about the human rejecting: the fence requires a person for judgment-like docs. If machine-signable docs (below threshold) include an actual judgment (FN), the person never sees it. The human-escalation quality matters for FPs' cost, not FN. FN is invisible in production — no alert fired. That's worth noting: in production, FNs are unobservable without audit. Any \"result\" measured on the bank says nothing about production FN rate, which depends on adversary behavior post-deployment. Medir: periodic adversarial audits (synthetic judgments injected into the pipeline to measure catch rate in vivo).\n\nA9. Threshold precision theater: three decimals (0.686) from a midpoint of two order statistics over 22 points; jackknife sensitivity: removing the 0.671 text or the 0.701 text moves the midpoint by ~±0.01+; so the number's uncertainty is of the same order as the 0.686-vs-0.697 difference being debated. ¿Cierto? Computable from existing data. Medir: jackknife/bootstrap the midpoint over the bank; report the interval; if the interval width ≥ 0.011, the debate between 0.686 and 0.697 is noise.\n\nLet me do the jackknife roughly: extremes: legit max 0.671, evasion min 0.701. Midpoint 0.686. Remove the 0.671 text → next legit max unknown (say ~0.65 from the ordinary set; we know narrow non-judgments max 0.616, but ordinary closures in complete bank — the 5 — we only know the max is 0.671; second highest unknown). If second-highest is 0.64, midpoint becomes 0.67. Remove the 0.701 evasion → next evasion min unknown; if 0.72, midpoint 0.6955 ≈ 0.70. So the midpoint ranges over ~[0.67, 0.70] under leave-one-out. The uncertainty (±0.015) exceeds the 0.011 difference between the two candidate thresholds. Boom — that's a self-contained refutation of the precision of the decision: the decision differs from its alternative by less than the sampling noise of the procedure that produced it. Medir: jackknife, report interval.\n\nAlso note attack on the rationale text: \"un umbral que solo separa los casos fáciles no separa nada\" — but 0.697 also catches all 9 evasions on the bank (0.701 > 0.697). So the rationale as stated mischaracterizes 0.697's bank performance: 0.697 does NOT fail on the bank; the argument for 0.686 is purely about hypothetical evasions in (0.686, 0.697). So the decision is: pay hypothetical FP cost (legit docs in [0.686,0.697) escalate) to buy hypothetical TP coverage (evasions in the same band). Neither density is known. It's a bet with zero data in the decisive band. THE measurement: populate the band. Generate/collect texts scoring 0.680–0.700 with both models from both classes (red team targeting the band specifically: attacker instructed \"get under 0.69 but above legit-looking\"; legit corpus mined for high-similarity ops closures) and count class composition of the band. If band is mostly legit → 0.686 wrong; mostly evasion → right.\n\nThis is essentially attack A2's measurement. Merge or keep separate? Keep A2 as \"validation has zero resolution: identical outcomes for any threshold in (0.671,0.701]\" and the band-population measurement. A9 (jackknife) is the precision attack. Both can stand.\n\nAlso should I mention: the decision's own numbers — the 22-case zero-discrepancy claim — includes B's threshold at 0.686? If B at 0.686 vs A at 0.70 never disagree, then on every case B's verdict = A's verdict. The legit at 0.671 (B-score) — what's its A-score? If A flagged it... they say zero discrepancies, so A didn't flag it either. And the difficult legit at 0.711 (B) — B at 0.686 flags it; so A must also flag it (no discrepancy). OK consistent.\n\nOne more possible attack: the \"margin\" of the narrow bank was 0.162 — the complete bank's 0.030 is 5× smaller; the decision implicitly trusts that the complete-bank extremes are the true extremes. But the complete bank's legit side gained only 7 texts over the narrow... and the highest legit 0.671 comes from the NEW ordinary 5 — meaning the narrow bank's non-judgment max was 0.616, and adding just 5 more samples raised the legit max by 0.055. Extrapolating: each new handful of legit samples raises the max. The 0.671 → next batch could raise to 0.70+. The observed drift per added sample is the empirical proof that n is too small. Medir: cumulative max curves — plot running max of legit similarity vs number of samples collected; if it hasn't plateaued (it can't be assessed to have plateaued with n=5 frontier-relevant), keep collecting until the running max stabilizes (e.g., 3 consecutive batches of 50 without new max... something operational).\n\nThat's a nice quantitative attack: the legit max moved 0.616 → 0.671 (+0.055) with just 5 new samples; the margin is 0.030, i.e., half the drift observed from ONE batch of 5. So the margin is smaller than the demonstrated sampling drift. Very concrete.\n\nSimilarly adversarial side: the attacker produced 9 texts with min 0.701... how many attempts did the attacker discard? Unknown — selection on the attacker side too. The 0.701 is their best PUBLISHED attempt.\n\nOK, I think I have plenty. Now structure per the contract:\n\nLine 1: Verdict. Is the decision sustainable? My role is to attack; the verdict should be my honest conclusion after the attacks. Given (i) validation has zero resolution over the debated interval, (ii) margin < demonstrated sampling drift, (iii) classes demonstrably overlap (difficult legitimates above the best evasion), (iv) adaptive adversary untested, (v) midpoint heuristic unrelated to the loss function — I conclude NO SOSTENIBLE. The decision 0.686 as \"the midpoint, validated by 9/9\" is not sustainable as justified; at best it's an unfalsifiable coin-flip between 0.686 and 0.697 with the evidence incapable of arbitrating.\n\nNote: I must be careful — verdict is about the DECISION (choosing 0.686 with that justification), not about whether some threshold exists. NO SOSTENIBLE.\n\nTable: numbered attacks, columns: #, attack, \"¿es cierto?\" (assessment: probable / desconocido / cierto-parcial / refutable-solo-con-datos), \"qué habría que medir\".\n\nThen reasoning, max 2000 words total. In Spanish (the whole prompt is Spanish). Keep it tight. Let me draft.\n\nWord budget: table maybe 500-600 words, reasoning 800-1000. Total under 2000. Spanish.\n\nLet me draft the table rows concisely:\n\n1. **El margen es una exclusión, no una separación.** Los 2 \"difíciles\" legítimos puntúan 0,711 y 0,768 — POR ENCIMA de la mejor evasión (0,701). Las clas