gits. Hmm, but maybe the 22 = 6 + 9 + 5 + 2 = 22! Yes: the 6 signature-reaching cases + 9 evasions + 5 ordinary + 2 difficult = 22. That reconciles. So the complete-bank \"margin\" computation used... the narrow bank's judgment min 0.778? But the complete bank says the lowest-similarity judgment is the evasion at 0.701. And the highest ordinary legit is 0.671. OK.\n\nSo the legitimate-side sample for the frontier is: 5 ordinary closures + maybe the 6 narrow cases (which are low similarity, 0.502–0.616 for non-judgments... wait the narrow non-judgments go up to 0.616, below 0.671). So the legitimate distribution near the frontier is represented by exactly FIVE ordinary closures. The p95 of legitimate similarity is estimated from n=5. That's the statistical heart: your 0.015 margin below threshold is 1/3 of the observed sample's inter-extreme range... The extreme of 5 samples estimates a quantile around 5/6 ≈ 83rd percentile, not a tail bound. The real p99 of legitimate closures could be 0.69, 0.70, we have no idea.\n\n(c) The two \"difficult on purpose\" cases at 0.768 and 0.711 counted as declared false positives. 0.711 is ABOVE the weakest evasion (0.701)! So a legitimate difficult closure scores higher than the best adversarial evasion. This destroys the notion of a margin: the classes overlap once you admit the difficult legitimates. The decision handles this by declaring them (\"the distinguishing cause isn't in the text\"). Fine — but then the reported \"margin +0.030\" is a margin computed AFTER excluding inconvenient points from the classes. The margin is conditional on a classification decision made by the same author who chose the threshold. If you include the 0.711 difficult legitimate in the \"legitimate\" class, the margin is NEGATIVE (0.711 > 0.701): there is no separating threshold, period. The system's answer is \"those cases are semantically indistinguishable from judgments; the distinguishing information isn't in the text; we accept 2 FPs.\" OK, but