sjunction) never disagree, the second fence adds zero measured coverage on this bank. The justification for adding model B would be robustness to model-A failures (different embedding spaces fail differently). But with n=22 and zero disagreement, you cannot distinguish \"B is redundant\" from \"B is redundant on the cases we happened to try.\" The claim of added value is untested; what's tested is non-contradiction. Also \"zero discrepancies\" on 22 cases gives a very weak bound on disagreement rate: rule of three, 95% upper bound on disagreement ~ 3/22 ≈ 13.6%. So the second fence could disagree on up to ~1 in 7 future texts and this test wouldn't have noticed. Actually wait — disagreement is fine in OR configuration (any flag escalates). The real question for a fence is FALSE NEGATIVES of both simultaneously (common-cause failure). Two embeddings models from... gemini-embedding-001 (Google) and text-multilingual-embedding-002 (also Google? \"text-multilingual-embedding-002\" is Vertex AI's multilingual embedding). Both from the same vendor, possibly similar training data/architecture family → correlated failures. Common-mode failure: the disjunction of two correlated detectors ≈ one detector. The measurement: adversarial texts optimized against model A only, then test whether model B catches them (transfer attack). If attacks transfer (they usually do within-family), the second fence is decorative. This is a key \"third problem\" the prompt hints at (\"¿hay un tercer problema?\").\n\n(e) Threshold scales: \"its cosine scale is different so reusing 0.70 would be inventing a number\" — fine, but then computing a midpoint of a 0.030 margin and reporting to three decimals (0.686) implies precision of ±0.001 that nothing in the procedure supports. Sensitivity analysis: how do the two candidate thresholds (0.686 vs 0.697) actually differ in outcomes? On this bank: both catch all 9 evasions (weakest 0.701 > 0.697). Both leave the 5 ordinary alone (highest 0.671 < 0.686 < 0.697). Both trigger the 2 difficult FPs. So ON THIS BANK THE DECISION IS A NO-OP: 0.686 and 0.697 produce IDENTICAL outcomes on all 22 cases! The only difference is hypothetical future cases falling between 0.686 and 0.697 — a 0.011 band with ZERO observed samples. The decision commits to covering an unobserved band based on... the claim that future evasions might land in [0.686, 0.697). But equally, future legitimate closures might land there too, and 0.686 flags them. Given no data in the band, the choice between 0.686 and 0.697 is a prior-driven bet, not an empirical result. The stated rationale (\"separa algo\") is unfalsifiable on current data. THE KEY MEASUREMENT: produce cases specifically in the 0.686–0.697 band (both classes) and see which class populates it. If it's populated by legitimate texts, 0.686 was wrong; if by evasions, right. This is a sharp attack: the decision is about a region with zero data points, and the bank cannot arbitrate it.\n\nWait, let me double check: does 0.697 catch the 0.701 evasion? 0.701 ≥ 0.697 → yes, caught, by 0.004. And the difficult legitimates at 0.711 and 0.768 trigger under both. The ordinary legit at 0.671 is below both. So indeed identical outcomes on all 22. The decision's actual effect is entirely deferred to unseen data. Excellent attack — it reframes: \"the measured result 9/9, 0 molestias is IDENTICAL under the rejected alternative, so the measured result provides zero evidence for choosing 0.686 over 0.697.\" The only differential evidence is the argument that the 0.004 margin at 0.697 is too thin — which is an argument about the future, i.e., about the adversarial tail. And if you believe adversarial tails are heavy, symmetry says push lower still — why stop at the midpoint? The midpoint is defended by symmetry, but the adversary is not symmetric with legitimate text. Under cost asymmetry (FN catastrophic, FP = a phone call), the rational move is: set threshold as low as the human-review budget tolerates. Nobody measured the FP cost or budget. The choice of midpoint is geometric aesthetics, not risk analysis.\n\nHmm, but careful: lowering the threshold indefinitely eventually flags ordinary ops text (0.671, then 0.64...). The budget constraint is real. But it's UNMEASURED. So the attack: \"0.686 was chosen by a symmetry heuristic; the two quantities that actually determine the optimum (cost of a missed judgment, tolerable rate of human escalations on legitimate volume) were never estimated; therefore the operating point is not derived from the system's loss function.\" Measurement: estimate production base rates — what fraction of daily documents are near-frontier legit (0.65–0.69)? If 5% of 10k docs/day land in [0.686, 0.697), the choice adds X human calls/day; if near-frontier is 0.001%, it's free. Without those base rates, neither 0.686 nor 0.697 is defensible relative to the other.\n\n(f) The bank's provenance: narrow bank written by the system's author. Author-written tests have the author's blind spots. The attacker texts were written by \"un atacante externo\" with explicit brief — good — but ONE attacker. The 2 difficult legitimates were \"difficult on purpose\" — author-imagined difficulty again. Everything routes through one head. Measurement: red-team with N independent attackers, ideally with feedback (adaptive), and collect legitimate closure corpus from real production history (not authored), measure the real similarity histogram.\n\n(g) Also: threshold validated with zero discrepancies — but were the fences evaluated at their FINAL thresholds jointly? The narrow-bank midpoint for A (0.70) was presumably set earlier on a similar procedure. Circular stacking: A's 0.70 was likely also a midpoint of some bank. The compound system inherits two selection biases.\n\n(h) Another angle: cosine similarity of a multilingual embedding across languages — the evasion was \"evasión ES\" (Spanish). Are there other languages in production? A threshold calibrated on Spanish+attacker-Spanish may not transfer to Catalan, English, Galician... The bank is 22 Spanish(?) cases; multilingual embedding models have per-language score offsets. Measurement: repeat bank in each production language; check whether 0.686 holds or whether per-language thresholds are needed. If the org signs documents in more than one language, the single number is under-determined. This is a real, concrete attack.\n\n(i) The declared FPs \"dicen 'saldo en cero' y 'expediente cerrado'... la causa que los distingue no está en el texto.\" This is a big concession: the system admits text-only features cannot separate some legit from judgment. If the distinguishing cause isn't in the text, where is it? Metadata (process type, document class, sender). The fences ignore it. An attacker who knows that \"expediente cerrado\" scores 0.711 legitimately can craft a judgment phrased as closure-vocabulary... wait, attacker wants LOW score, so attacker wants to look like ordinary ops text, not like \"expediente cerrado\" (which is high). Fine. But the existence of legit texts at 0.711+ means the human-escalation path is routinely triggered by legit traffic whose base rate is unknown (n=2 authored). If such phrasing is common in production, the fence cries wolf constantly, and — here's the systemic risk — alert fatigue: if \"persona requerida\" becomes routine noise, the human review degrades into rubber-stamping, and the fence's safety becomes nominal. That's a mechanism-level attack: the safety depends on the human being a real check, and unmeasured FP base rates determine whether the human remains a check. Measurement: production pilot measuring FP rate per week, and time-to-decision / reversal rate of human reviews over time.\n\n(j) Statistical framing of \"margin 0.030 with 22 points\": the extremes are order statistics. Rough confidence: the true min of the adversarial distribution lies below 0.701 with high probability; the true max of legit above 0.671. There's a real chance the true distributions overlap at 0.686 (indeed the difficult legitimates at 0.711 prove overlap once you include them). The \"margin\" exists only after excluding the inconvenient points. Honest statement: the classes OVERLAP in embedding space; the threshold picks an operating point inside the overlap zone; the \"9/9, 0 molestias\" is the overlap artfully sampled. That's maybe the crispest formulation: the complete bank as presented doesn't show separation; it shows that after moving 2 points to a \"declared FP\" drawer, the remaining 20 have a 0.030 gap. The decision to exclude is the decision that creates the margin. And the exclusion criterion (\"the cause isn't in the text\") was not tested — it's asserted. Measurement: blind test — give the 2 difficult texts (plus judgments and ordinary) to independent annotators with only the text; if annotators can't separate them better than chance, the \"cause isn't in the text\" claim holds; if they can, the text DOES contain the signal and the fence (not the ontology) is what's failing.\n\nThat's a nice one.\n\n(k) Also the \"6 of 12 reach signature\" — half the narrow bank never exercises the fence's decision path. Selection of what's in the bank is again author-driven.\n\n(l) Question 4 in the prompt: \"cero discrepancias = safe confirmation or proof of no added value?\" I covered: on this bank B is redundant; the disjunction only helps if A and B fail independently; both are Google-family embeddings, plausibly trained on similar corpora → correlated; and the OR only adds value for cases where B catches what A misses — zero observed. So B's marginal benefit is unmeasured, and its COST is measured: B at 0.686 adds false positives... wait does it? In OR, B triggering adds escalation. On this bank, zero discrepancies means B never triggered differently... hmm, \"zero discrepancies between the two fences\" — meaning on all 22, both fences gave the same verdict? Then B changed nothing at all on the bank: no added catches