xtremes are the true extremes. But the complete bank's legit side gained only 7 texts over the narrow... and the highest legit 0.671 comes from the NEW ordinary 5 — meaning the narrow bank's non-judgment max was 0.616, and adding just 5 more samples raised the legit max by 0.055. Extrapolating: each new handful of legit samples raises the max. The 0.671 → next batch could raise to 0.70+. The observed drift per added sample is the empirical proof that n is too small. Medir: cumulative max curves — plot running max of legit similarity vs number of samples collected; if it hasn't plateaued (it can't be assessed to have plateaued with n=5 frontier-relevant), keep collecting until the running max stabilizes (e.g., 3 consecutive batches of 50 without new max... something operational).\n\nThat's a nice quantitative attack: the legit max moved 0.616 → 0.671 (+0.055) with just 5 new samples; the margin is 0.030, i.e., half the drift observed from ONE batch of 5. So the margin is smaller than the demonstrated sampling drift. Very concrete.\n\nSimilarly adversarial side: the attacker produced 9 texts with min 0.701... how many attempts did the attacker discard? Unknown — selection on the attacker side too. The 0.701 is their best PUBLISHED attempt.\n\nOK, I think I have plenty. Now structure per the contract:\n\nLine 1: Verdict. Is the decision sustainable? My role is to attack; the verdict should be my honest conclusion after the attacks. Given (i) validation has zero resolution over the debated interval, (ii) margin < demonstrated sampling drift, (iii) classes demonstrably overlap (difficult legitimates above the best evasion), (iv) adaptive adversary untested, (v) midpoint heuristic unrelated to the loss function — I conclude NO SOSTENIBLE. The decision 0.686 as \"the midpoint, validated by 9/9\" is not sustainable as justified; at best it's an unfalsifiable coin-flip between 0.686 and 0.697 with the evidence incapable of arbitrating.\n\nNote: I must be careful — verdict is